<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="Rubisco shunt" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5723" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>Oil is a major carbon reserve in most plant seeds. Conversion of carbohydrates to oil is generally achieved by |FRAME:GLYCOLYSIS| and |FRAME:PWY-1042|. Pyruvate, the end product of glycolysis, is further decarboxylated to acetyl-CoA, the precursor for fatty acid and oil biosynthesis. However, in the process, one-third of carbon is lost as CO2. An elegant experiment recently reported by Schwender et. al.|CITS: [15592419]|, illustrated the existence of a Rubisco shunt. Comparing to glycolysis, the Rubisco shunt results in an increase of carbon conversion efficiency, yielding 20% more acetyl-CoA with 40% less carbon loss. They further showed that in oilseed rape, the Rubisco shunt is responsible for the production of 37% -75% of phosphoglycerate in the developing embryo. There are three stages in the Rubisco shunt. Stage I is composed of the non-oxidative pentose phosphate pathway (in reverse direction of what is shown in |FRAME:NONOXIPENT-PWY|), where a series re-arrangement of carbon skeleton converts the six-carbon sugar fructose-6-phosphate to the five-carbon sugar ribulose-5-phosphate. Ribulose-5-phosphate is further converted to ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate. The net change is: 5 fructose-6-phosphate = 6 ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate Stage II is catalyzed by the carboxylase activity of Rubisco, which fixes one molecule CO2 with one molecule ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate yielding two molecules three-carbon phosphoglycerate. The net change is: 6 ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate + 6 CO2 = 12 phosphoglycerate In stage III, phosphoglycerate is converted to pyruvate via the later steps of glycolysis. Pyruvate is further metabolized to acetyl-CoA which fuels fatty acid biosynthesis (|FRAME:PWY-4381| and seed oil (|FRAME:TRIGLSYN-PWY|) accumulation. The net change is: 12 phosphoglycerate = 12 acetyl-CoA + 12 CO2 Overall, the net carbon stoichiometry via Rubisco shunt is: 5 fructose-6-phosphate = 12 acetyl-CoA + 6 CO2 Comparing to glycolysis: 5 fructose-6-phosphate = 10 acetyl-CoA + 10 CO2 Thus Rubisco shunt allows the conversion of carbohydrate into 20% more acetyl-CoA than glycolysis, and has 40% less carbon loss as CO2. Note that there is no active |FRAME:CALVIN-PWY| following the Rubisco step in developing seeds. This allows the continuation of Rubisco shunt to stage III. The whole pathway is located in the plastid.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE" />
    <Author name="zhang" />
  </Authors>
